    "md5": "7fe7356053d4a52cea114ae6770b5b3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1677,
    "chunk_text": "In Sri Lanka, there is an indigenous system of traditional medicine known as Hela Vedakama, or Deshiya Chikitsa. Although it has been influenced to some extent by Indian Ayurveda, it encompasses medicinal formulations and treatment methods that are unique to Sri Lanka, including traditional hereditary practices for snakebites, eye diseases, fractures and dislocations, burns and scalds, tumours and wounds, rabies, mental health conditions and skin diseases.\n\n(ii) This situation has arisen because the legislative process was largely been carried out among the members of the Ayurvedic Medical Council, resulting in inadequate representation for traditional medical practitioners and a failure to consider the views of experienced traditional medical practitioners.\n\n(iii) Yes. The National Institute of Indigenous\n\nMedicine conducts training programmes to develop the knowledge of indigenous traditional medical practitioners at an\n\ninstitutional level. In addition, institutional education in certain fields can be obtained through the faculties of indigenous medicine established under government universities such as the Faculty of Indigenous Medicine of the University of Colombo and the Gampaha Wickramarachchi Ayurveda University.\n\n(iv) No.\n\nUniversity and institutional education primarily focuses on academic courses in Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani medicine. However, the inherited and often closely guarded experiential knowledge of indigenous traditional medicine in Sri Lanka, including specialized traditional remedies and practical knowledge acquired through guru-kula traditions - tacit knowledge - cannot be fully acquired solely through university curricula."
